<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert F. Kennedy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Robert Francis Kennedy, RFK</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Francis Kennedy was an American politician and lawyer who served as the 64th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> attorney general from 1961 to 1964 and as a senator from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> from 1965 until his assassination in 1968. He managed the campaigns of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> and was his closest adviser, and as attorney general he supported the civil rights movement and fought organised crime. As a senator he opposed US involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and sponsored legislation against poverty. A leading candidate for the Democratic presidential nomination in 1968, he was shot by Sirhan Sirhan shortly after winning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> primary and died 25 hours later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-f-kennedy.jpg" alt="Robert F. Kennedy" width="330" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harry Warnecke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_F._Kennedy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kennedy was born in Brookline, Massachusetts, on 20 November 1925, the seventh of the nine children of Joseph P. Kennedy Sr., a politician and businessman, and Rose Fitzgerald Kennedy. All four of his grandparents were children of Irish immigrants, and both parents came from prominent Irish-American families active in the Massachusetts Democratic Party. He enlisted in the United States Naval Reserve in 1943, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> as a junior in 1946, and graduated in 1948 with a degree in political science.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After Harvard, Kennedy toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> as a correspondent for The Boston Post. He earned his law degree at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, worked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Justice, and resigned on 6 June 1952 to manage his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a>'s campaign for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-senate">United States Senate</a>. He was chief counsel of the Senate Labor Rackets Committee from 1957 to 1959, where he publicly challenged the Teamsters president Jimmy Hoffa.</p><p>President-elect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> appointed him attorney general in 1961, over criticism that he was inexperienced. He built the first coordinated program involving all 26 federal law enforcement agencies against organised crime, and convictions of organised crime figures rose by 800 percent during his term. He authorised the Federal Bureau of Investigation to wiretap <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> on a limited basis. He resigned on 3 September 1964 and won a New York Senate seat, defeating the Republican incumbent Kenneth Keating.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the Senate Kennedy opposed American involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> and sponsored legislation to draw private business to poor communities. After the Tet Offensive of early 1968 he entered the race for the Democratic presidential nomination. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and South Dakota primaries on 4 June. Shortly after midnight he left a speech at the Ambassador Hotel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> through the kitchen, where Sirhan Sirhan, a 24-year-old Palestinian, shot him three times. He died 25 hours later and was buried near his brother at Arlington National Cemetery.</p><p>He married Ethel Skakel in 1950, and they had eleven children. His books include The Enemy Within (1960) and Thirteen Days, an account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a> published after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert F. Kennedy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Farmworkers sang it in Spanish in the strikes and grape boycotts of the late 1960s, and <b>Robert F. Kennedy</b> led anti-apartheid crowds in it from the roof of his car in South Africa in 1966.</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-shall-overcome">We Shall Overcome</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Brookline, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.33,-71.12&amp;year=1952" aria-label="Brookline on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Robert F. Kennedy"><image href="/maps/globes/g300n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4301 3919h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4301 3919h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.33,-71.12&amp;year=1952">What was here in 1952? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
